# Reference Video Analysis

**Video used:** "3 - What is LangChain.mp4"

## What I did

Watched the video a few times and noted where the look and pacing change — background color,
how much text/detail is on screen, how long each scene stays up before switching.

## What I found

The video switches between three visual "moods" rather than having one single look:

1. **Light background, explanatory** — used for most of the main narration. Plain background,
   text, sometimes a small icon. This is the default look, and it's on screen the longest.
2. **Dark background, high contrast** — used at the very start (intro), once in the middle for
   what looks like a diagram or code snippet, and again at the end (closing). Feels like it's
   used to signal "this part is different" from the regular narration.
3. **Very bright, almost empty** — used once, for a long stretch, just one short line of big
   text on screen. Reads like a key-takeaway moment held for emphasis.

Scenes don't change very often — most stay up for 20-35+ seconds — so this is closer to a
slide-plus-voiceover style than fast-cut content, which lines up with what the PRD describes.


## Still need to confirm

- Exact on-screen copy and font used per scene (need the brand guideline doc, wasn't provided)
- Exact colors — I can tell light vs. dark but not exact hex values
- Whether the dark scenes are diagrams, code snippets, or a mix of both

## How this maps to the layout catalogue

- Light mode → **Text & Explanation**
- Dark mode → **Image & Media** (diagrams/code) and **Closing/CTA**
- Bright/minimal mode → **Key Takeaway**

The other required layouts (Title, Narrator/Presenter, Comparison, Step-by-step) weren't
literally present as their own segments in this one video — a 3-minute explainer isn't going
to have a side-by-side comparison scene, for example. Those are built using the same visual
language (same colors, spacing, typography) rather than something I directly saw, so they
should be sanity-checked once more reference videos are available.